# Prefer CLIs & Generators

When a project or ecosystem CLI can create, install, update, or scaffold an artifact, **run that CLI** instead of writing the files by hand.

## Prefer CLI when

- Adding UI from a registry (e.g. `pnpm dlx shadcn@latest add …` / `npx shadcn@latest …`)
- Package install / workspace scripts (`pnpm add`, `pnpm exec`, package.json scripts)
- Schema tooling that owns generated output (e.g. Prisma migrate / generate)
- Any scaffold/init/update command documented by a loaded skill (follow that skill’s CLI)

Use the repo’s package runner (`pnpm` preferred when lockfile/scripts use it; otherwise `npx` / `pnpm dlx` as the skill specifies).

## Hand-write when

- Small edits to existing files (one-liners, wiring, bugfixes)
- Project-specific business logic, routes, services, or tests with no generator
- The CLI does not exist, fails, or the user asks for a manual approach

## Token discipline

- Prefer CLI for **large boilerplate** so the model does not stream full file bodies.
- Skip heavy CLI discovery (`info --json`, multi-doc fetches) for tiny local tweaks.
- After generate: read the new files, then wire them into port-vault patterns (`api--contracts.mdc`, `frontend--apps.mdc`, etc.).

## Do not

- Invent component/scaffold source that a CLI would produce identically
- Fetch raw upstream files from GitHub when the official CLI can add/update them
- Bypass skill CLI workflows (especially `.agents/skills/shadcn/`) when that skill applies
